# TypeScript with components

The **Initialise with components** checkbox installs one supported application
stack. All TypeScript code stays under this directory: `apps/web` owns Next.js
routes, authentication, persistence and product features; `packages/ui` owns
React components, layouts, Tailwind tokens and their Storybook catalog.

## Start locally

From the generated repository root, in its pinned `devenv shell`:

```bash
just setup
just dev
```

Setup installs the committed Bun lock and creates a private, ignored
`apps/web/.env.local` once. It never replaces an existing configuration. `just dev`
starts local PostgreSQL and Mailpit, applies committed migrations, then starts
Next.js. Open the address printed by Next.js (normally <http://localhost:3000>).
Create a local account, add a project, then edit, filter or delete it. Records are
scoped to the signed-in user. No hosted account or API key is needed.

In another terminal, use `just typescript::storybook` to browse the same components
and themes. `just services-down` stops the local database and email preview.
Devenv may allocate available ports; its exported `DATABASE_URL` and `SMTP_PORT`
take precedence over the local configuration. Mailpit shows the address of its
local inbox when services start.

## Included stack

| Concern | Supported implementation |
| --- | --- |
| Application | Next.js App Router, React, Node.js 24 |
| Packages and commands | Bun workspaces, Just, frozen lock |
| UI | Official shadcn Base UI sources, Tailwind CSS, Lucide, Sonner |
| Shared UX | App shell, page headers, empty states, tables, charts, light/dark themes |
| Forms | React Hook Form and Zod |
| Persistence | PostgreSQL and Drizzle, committed SQL migrations |
| Accounts | Self-hosted Better Auth with database sessions and local SMTP |
| Quality | Strict TypeScript, Biome, Knip, enforced UI import boundaries |
| Tests | Vitest, Storybook browser interactions/accessibility, Playwright E2E and screenshots |

Packages are pinned in manifests and `bun.lock`. Bun installs and runs scripts;
Next.js and the development tools execute with Node. No task graph or second
component system is required.

## Work and validate

```bash
just services-up
just typescript::db-migrate
just typescript::browser-setup
just check
```

Nix supplies Chromium. Outside Nix, `browser-setup` explicitly downloads the
browser matching the pinned Playwright release. Ordinary checks never fetch it.
The complete gate runs lint, architecture checks, strict types, unit tests,
production and Storybook builds, component interactions, browser journeys,
accessibility, and visual regression. Services and migrations must be prepared
first. Checks write only ignored build/test output; they never update source,
locks, migrations or image baselines.

Use `just typescript::test` for fast unit tests, `test-stories` for the component
suite, `test-e2e` for the built application, or `test-visual` for built Storybook.
Browser failures leave traces and HTML reports in `src/typescript/test-results`
and `src/typescript/playwright-report`. Run `just typescript::fmt` to explicitly
format sources.

Screenshot baselines target Linux x86-64. On ARM, the same stories still run
interaction, accessibility and layout checks; pixel comparisons are explicitly
skipped. To intentionally accept a design change, run
`just typescript::visual-update` on Linux x86-64 and review the image diff before
committing. Missing baselines are errors in ordinary checks.

## Make a feature

Start with the projects feature under `apps/web/src/features/projects`. Validate
incoming data with Zod; authenticate and check ownership in every server mutation.
Compose existing UI instead of creating new colors or behavior implementations.
The UI package exposes direct imports and contains no Next.js, authentication or
database dependencies. Its [catalog and contribution guide](packages/ui/README.md)
explain how to add primitives and stories. Read the [application guide](apps/web/README.md)
for account flows, email delivery, migrations and deployment.

## Updates

This is one whole stack, not independent choices for each library. Same-variant
Copier upgrades preserve saved selections and surface authored-source conflicts
for review. Switching an existing TypeScript project between the lean and
components variants is deliberately rejected: generate a fresh project and
migrate reviewed code and data explicitly. This prevents Copier from removing
an authored library or application when the starter structure changes.

See [architecture](ARCHITECTURE.md) for ownership and theme conventions.
